    ],
    'en' => [
        'title' => 'Free Online Rule of Three Calculator | uToolsGo',
        'meta'  => 'Solve a simple rule of three (direct or inverse proportion) online for free, with the result and calculation explained.',
        'h1'    => 'Rule of Three Calculator',
        'subtitle' => 'Solve simple direct or inverse proportions in seconds.',
        'fieldLabels' => ['a' => 'A', 'b' => 'B', 'c' => 'C', 'tipo' => 'Proportion type'],
        'optionLabels' => ['direta' => 'Direct (A is to B as C is to X)', 'inversa' => 'Inverse (A is to B as X is to C)'],
        'strings' => [
            'resultTemplate' => 'X = {result}', 'errorDivZero' => 'Division by zero — please check the values entered.',
            'error' => 'Please fill in all three values with valid, non-zero numbers where applicable.',
        ],
        'article' => '<h2>What is the rule of three</h2><p>The rule of three is used to solve proportionality problems between two or more quantities: given that A is to B as C is to an unknown value X, the rule of three finds X.</p><h2>Direct proportion</h2><p>When the quantities are directly proportional (as one increases, the other increases in the same ratio): <strong>X = (B × C) / A</strong>. Example: if 3 items cost $30, how much do 7 items cost? X = (30 × 7) / 3 = $70.</p><h2>Inverse proportion</h2><p>When the quantities are inversely proportional (as one increases, the other decreases in the same ratio): <strong>X = (A × B) / C</strong>. Example: if 4 workers finish a job in 12 days, how many days would 6 workers take? X = (4 × 12) / 6 = 8 days.</p><h2>How to tell direct from inverse</h2><p>Ask yourself: as one quantity increases, does the other also increase (direct) or decrease (inverse)? Price and quantity is usually direct; number of workers and time to finish a job is usually inverse.</p>',
