        'notice' => 'For the trapezoid, an isosceles trapezoid is assumed (both non-parallel sides have the same length) — enter just one leg value.',
        'article' => '<h2>Formulas used</h2><p><strong>Square</strong>: area = side², perimeter = 4 × side. <strong>Rectangle</strong>: area = base × height, perimeter = 2 × (base + height). <strong>Triangle</strong> (from the 3 sides, Heron\'s formula): semi-perimeter s = (a+b+c)/2, area = √(s(s-a)(s-b)(s-c)), perimeter = a+b+c. <strong>Circle</strong>: area = π × radius², perimeter (circumference) = 2 × π × radius. <strong>Isosceles trapezoid</strong>: area = ((longer base + shorter base) / 2) × height, perimeter = longer base + shorter base + 2 × leg. <strong>Parallelogram</strong>: area = base × height, perimeter = 2 × (base + side). <strong>Rhombus</strong>: area = (longer diagonal × shorter diagonal) / 2, perimeter = 4 × side (the side is derived from the diagonals via the Pythagorean theorem).</p><h2>Area unit</h2><p>If measurements are entered in meters, area comes out in square meters (m²) and perimeter in meters (m) — the calculator doesn\'t convert units, so always use the same unit across all fields of a shape.</p>',
        'faq' => [
            ['q' => 'Why does the triangle ask for 3 sides instead of base and height?', 'a' => 'Because with the 3 sides (Heron\'s formula) you can calculate the area of any triangle without needing to measure the height, which is usually harder to get in practice than measuring the sides.'],
            ['q' => 'Does it work for a trapezoid with different (non-isosceles) legs?', 'a' => 'The area works for any trapezoid, since it only depends on the bases and the height. The perimeter, however, assumes both non-parallel sides are equal (isosceles trapezoid) — for an irregular trapezoid, manually add up the 4 real sides.'],
