        ],
        'article' => '<h2>The US Navy method</h2><p>Developed by the United States Navy as a practical alternative to hydrostatic weighing (the "gold standard" at the time, but impractical at scale), this method estimates body fat percentage using just a measuring tape, taking the circumference of the neck, waist and — for women — hip. Validation studies show an error margin of about 3-4 percentage points compared to more precise methods (like DEXA), which is fine for personal tracking but not for clinical diagnosis.</p><h2>How to measure correctly</h2><p><strong>Neck:</strong> just below the larynx, with the tape sloping slightly downward at the front. <strong>Waist:</strong> men measure at navel height; women measure at the narrowest point of the torso. <strong>Hip</strong> (women): at the widest point of the glutes. Measure without sucking in your stomach and with the tape level, not pressing into the skin.</p><p><em>This is an estimate and doesn\'t replace a professional body composition assessment (bioimpedance, DEXA, or skinfold measurements done by a trained professional).</em></p>',
        'faq' => [
            ['q' => 'Why is the formula different for men and women?', 'a' => 'Men and women store fat differently (android vs. gynoid distribution) and have different healthy body fat ranges — that\'s why the Navy formula uses different measurements and coefficients for each sex, including hip only in the female calculation.'],
            ['q' => 'Is this method as accurate as bioimpedance or DEXA?', 'a' => 'No — it\'s a practical estimate with a larger error margin (about 3-4 percentage points), but it has the advantage of needing no equipment beyond a measuring tape, which makes it useful for tracking trends over time, even if not exact in absolute value.'],
        ],
    ],
