        'fieldLabels' => ['rendaMensal' => 'Monthly net income'],
        'strings' => [
            'needsLabel' => 'Needs', 'wantsLabel' => 'Wants', 'savingsLabel' => 'Savings & investments', 'totalLabel' => 'Total income',
            'error' => 'Please enter a valid monthly income.',
        ],
        'article' => '<h2>What is the 50/30/20 rule</h2><p>The 50/30/20 rule is a simple personal budgeting method popularized by U.S. senator Elizabeth Warren. It splits your monthly net income (after taxes) into three buckets: 50% for needs, 30% for wants and 20% for savings and debt payoff.</p><h2>What goes in each category</h2><p><strong>Needs (50%):</strong> rent or mortgage, utilities, groceries, commuting costs, health insurance and minimum debt payments. These are essential expenses you would still have even on a lower income.</p><p><strong>Wants (30%):</strong> entertainment, streaming subscriptions, dining out, non-essential clothing, hobbies and travel. These improve your quality of life but aren\'t strictly necessary.</p><p><strong>Savings & investments (20%):</strong> emergency fund, retirement, investments, and any extra debt payments beyond the minimum.</p><h2>How to use the result</h2><p>If your essential spending is above 50% of your income today, that\'s a warning sign — consider reviewing housing, transportation or high-interest debt. The ratio is a flexible starting point, not a strict rule: adapt it to your goals and local cost of living.</p>',
        'faq' => [
            ['q' => 'Does the 50/30/20 rule work for any income level?', 'a' => 'It works well as a starting point, but lower earners may need to spend more than 50% on needs, while higher earners can often save well beyond 20%. Use it as a reference, not a law.'],
            ['q' => 'Which category do debts fall into?', 'a' => 'Minimum debt payments (credit card, loan installments) fall under needs. Any extra amount paid to pay off debt faster falls under the savings slice (20%).'],
            ['q' => 'Should I use gross or net income?', 'a' => 'Always use net income (after taxes and mandatory deductions), since that\'s the amount that actually lands in your account to spend.'],
            ['q' => 'What if I can\'t save 20%?', 'a' => 'Start with whatever percentage is realistic, even 5% or 10%, and increase it gradually as you cut back on wants or grow your income.'],
