        'subtitle' => 'Three words, same sound, different jobs: there (place), their (possession), they\'re (they are).',
        'article' => '<h2>There: a place</h2><p><strong>There</strong> refers to a location or is used to introduce a sentence ("there is/are"): "Put the box over there." "There are three options."</p><h2>Their: possession</h2><p><strong>Their</strong> shows that something belongs to a group of people: "They forgot their umbrellas." "It\'s their decision."</p><h2>They\'re: contraction of "they are"</h2><p><strong>They\'re</strong> is short for "they are": "They\'re coming to dinner." "They\'re not ready yet."</p><h2>The trick</h2><p>Try replacing the word with "they are" — if it fits, use "they\'re." If the sentence is about ownership, use "their." Otherwise (location or "there is/are"), use "there."</p>',
