        'meta' => '"Farther" is for physical distance; "further" is for figurative or additional degree. Learn the distinction with examples.',
        'h1' => '"Farther" vs. "Further": What\'s the Difference?',
        'subtitle' => '"Farther" = physical distance you can measure; "further" = figurative distance, extent, or "additional."',
        'article' => '<h2>The basic rule</h2><p><strong>Farther</strong> is traditionally used for literal, physical distance: "The store is farther than I thought." <strong>Further</strong> is used for figurative distance, degree, or extent, and also means "additional": "We need further discussion." "Nothing could be further from the truth."</p><h2>The trick</h2><p>Farther has "far" in it — think of physical distance you could measure with a ruler or a map. Further is broader — used for anything abstract, or when you mean "more"/"additional."</p><h2>Examples</h2><p>Farther: "He can throw the ball farther than me." "How much farther is it to the beach?"</p><p>Further: "Let\'s discuss this further." "For further information, contact us." "She took her argument further than necessary."</p><h2>In practice</h2><p>Many English speakers (and even style guides) use "further" for both meanings, and it\'s rarely considered wrong. "Farther" is the safer, more precise choice specifically for literal distance.</p>',
