        'subtitle' => '"Principal" = main, or the head of a school, or loan amount; "principle" = a rule or belief.',
        'article' => '<h2>Principal: main, or a person in charge</h2><p><strong>Principal</strong> (ends in -pal) has two common uses: as an adjective meaning "main" or "most important" ("the principal reason"), and as a noun for a person in charge, like a school principal, or the original amount of a loan (before interest): "she paid off the principal."</p><h2>Principle: a rule or belief</h2><p><strong>Principle</strong> (ends in -ple) is a noun meaning a fundamental rule, law, or belief: "honesty is one of his core principles." "the principle of supply and demand."</p><h2>The trick</h2><p>The school principal is your <strong>pal</strong> (friend) — principal ends in "-pal." Principle ends in "-ple," like "ruLE" — think of a rule or law.</p><h2>Examples</h2><p>Principal: "the principal cause of the delay," "she called the school principal," "pay down the loan principal."</p><p>Principle: "it\'s against my principles," "the basic principles of physics."</p>',
