        'article' => '<h2>What is spaced repetition</h2><p>Spaced repetition is a study technique based on Hermann Ebbinghaus\'s "forgetting curve": reviewing material at increasing time intervals is far more effective for long-term retention than cramming everything at once.</p><h2>The intervals used</h2><p>This planner suggests reviews at <strong>1, 3, 7, 15 and 30 days</strong> after the initial study session — a classic progression used by flashcard systems like Anki and by many exam-prep methods.</p><h2>How to use it</h2><p>Pick the date you studied (or will study) the material, and the planner generates the five suggested review dates.</p><h2>Tip</h2><p>Each review doesn\'t need to be long — reread your notes, redo a few exercises, or try explaining the material from memory. The goal is to reactivate the information right before it would otherwise be forgotten.</p>',
