        'article' => '<h2>How to calculate the amount of paint</h2><p>The basic formula is: <strong>Liters = (area × number of coats ÷ paint coverage) × loss margin</strong>. Coverage varies by paint type and surface — the values used here are typical market averages.</p><h2>Average coverage by paint type</h2><table><tr><th>Type</th><th>Average coverage</th></tr><tr><td>Latex/emulsion</td><td>~6 m²/liter per coat</td></tr><tr><td>Acrylic</td><td>~5 m²/liter per coat</td></tr><tr><td>Enamel</td><td>~8 m²/liter per coat</td></tr></table><h2>Why 2 coats?</h2><p>Most manufacturers recommend at least two coats for uniform coverage, especially when painting over a different color or an unpainted wall.</p><h2>Tip</h2><p>Real coverage can vary depending on wall texture, color (dark/vivid colors sometimes need extra coats) and application method (roller vs. brush vs. sprayer).</p>',
