        ],
        'article' => '<h2>What is a concrete "mix ratio"</h2><p>The mix ratio is the proportion of cement, sand and gravel used in the mix — for example, 1:2:3 means 1 part cement to 2 parts sand to 3 parts gravel. The 1:2:3 ratio is one of the most common for standard structural concrete (roughly 20-25 MPa).</p><h2>How the calculation works</h2><p>The total volume is split between cement, sand and gravel, proportionally to the mix ratio. Cement volume is converted to 50 kg bags (assuming ~0.033 m³ per bag). Water is estimated using an approximate water/cement ratio of 0.55.</p><h2>Common mix ratios</h2><table><tr><th>Ratio</th><th>Typical use</th></tr><tr><td>1:2:3</td><td>Standard structural concrete (beams, slabs, columns)</td></tr><tr><td>1:3:3</td><td>Screed, sidewalks</td></tr><tr><td>1:2:4</td><td>Light foundations, floors</td></tr></table><h2>Note</h2><p>For structures requiring a specific compressive strength, consult a structural engineer — the ideal mix depends on the aggregate type, cement used and the structure\'s purpose.</p>',
