        'article' => '<h2>How it works</h2><p>Enter any year and this calculator instantly shows every US federal holiday for that year — including moving-date holidays like Thanksgiving, Memorial Day and Labor Day, and the weekend-observance shift for fixed-date holidays — entirely in your browser.</p><h2>Weekday-rule vs. fixed-date holidays</h2><p>Six federal holidays are defined by a weekday rule (e.g. Thanksgiving is always the fourth Thursday of November, Memorial Day is always the last Monday of May), so they always land on a weekday. The other five (New Year\'s Day, Juneteenth, Independence Day, Veterans Day, Christmas) are fixed calendar dates and shift to the nearest weekday when they fall on a Saturday or Sunday, following the federal OPM observance rule.</p><h2>Already have dedicated pages for upcoming years</h2><p>If you\'re looking for 2025–2030 specifically, we have dedicated pages with the full table and extra context: <a href="/holidays/us-federal-holidays-2025/">2025</a>, <a href="/holidays/us-federal-holidays-2026/">2026</a>, <a href="/holidays/us-federal-holidays-2027/">2027</a>, <a href="/holidays/us-federal-holidays-2028/">2028</a>, <a href="/holidays/us-federal-holidays-2029/">2029</a> and <a href="/holidays/us-federal-holidays-2030/">2030</a>. For any other year, use the calculator above.</p>',
        'faq' => [
            ['q' => 'Does this work for any year?', 'a' => 'Yes — it works for any year between 1900 and 2200, applying the correct weekday rules and weekend-observance shifts for that year.'],
            ['q' => 'Why does New Year\'s Day sometimes show a date in the previous year?', 'a' => 'When January 1st falls on a Saturday, federal employees observe the holiday the preceding Friday (December 31st of the prior year), following the official OPM weekend-observance rule.'],
            ['q' => 'Does it include state holidays?', 'a' => 'No — it only shows the 11 federal holidays recognized nationwide. State and local government holidays vary and aren\'t included.'],
        ],
    ],
