            'sharpFromLabel' => 'Focused at the hyperfocal, everything is sharp starting from',
        ],
        'article' => '<h2>What it\'s for</h2><p>The hyperfocal distance is the focus distance that maximizes depth of field: focusing exactly on it makes everything from half that distance out to infinity appear acceptably sharp. It\'s a classic landscape photography technique used to keep both foreground and background in focus.</p><h2>Formula used</h2><p>We use the standard formula <code>H = f²/(N·c) + f</code>, where f is the focal length, N is the f-number (aperture) and c is the sensor\'s circle of confusion — the same calculation used in our <a href="/en/calculators/depth-of-field-calculator/">depth of field calculator</a>, isolated here to focus just on the hyperfocal distance.</p><h2>How to use it in practice</h2><p>After calculating the hyperfocal distance, manually set focus (or use the lens\'s distance scale) to that distance. Everything from roughly H/2 out to the horizon will appear sharp — so the smaller the hyperfocal distance, the easier it is to get an entire scene in focus.</p><h2>Why the hyperfocal changes with aperture and sensor</h2><p>Smaller apertures (higher f-numbers) reduce the hyperfocal distance, since they increase overall depth of field. Smaller sensors (with a smaller circle of confusion) also tend to have different hyperfocal distances than full-frame cameras at the same focal length and aperture.</p><h2>Limitations</h2><p>The calculated value depends on the standard circle of confusion used for each sensor format — a practical convention, not a measurement of your specific lens\'s actual sharpness.</p>',
        'faq' => [
            ['q' => 'What does "focusing at the hyperfocal" mean?', 'a' => 'It means manually setting the lens focus to the calculated hyperfocal distance instead of focusing on a specific subject — this maximizes the area of the scene that appears sharp, from foreground to background.'],
            ['q' => 'Why is this technique common in landscape photography?', 'a' => 'Because landscapes often have points of interest both close and far away, and the hyperfocal distance lets you keep both sharp in the same shot without resorting to focus stacking.'],
            ['q' => 'Does the hyperfocal distance change if I switch lenses?', 'a' => 'Yes — it depends directly on the focal length used, so each lens (or each zoom setting) has its own hyperfocal distance for a given aperture and sensor.'],
        ],
    ],
