        'article' => '<h2>What it\'s for</h2><p>Neutral density (ND) filters block a fixed amount of light without shifting the image\'s colors, letting you use much longer shutter speeds even in full daylight — the classic technique for smoothing moving water, streaking clouds or blurring crowds in long-exposure photos. This calculator gives you the new shutter speed once you add an ND filter to your original speed.</p><h2>Formula used</h2><p>Each ND filter blocks a fixed number of light stops, corresponding to its factor: <code>stops = log₂(ND_factor)</code>. The new shutter speed is <code>new_speed = original_speed × 2^stops</code>. For example, an ND8 filter (factor 8) blocks log₂(8) = 3 stops, multiplying the shutter speed by 2³ = 8.</p><h2>ND factor table used</h2><p>ND2 = 1 stop; ND4 = 2 stops; ND8 = 3 stops; ND16 = 4 stops; ND32 = 5 stops; ND64 = 6 stops; ND100 = 6.64 stops; ND400 = 8.64 stops; ND1000 (known as the "big stopper") = 10 stops; ND32000 = 15 stops. These follow the most common optical-density factor numbering on the market (ND followed by the light-blocking factor).</p><h2>Worked example</h2><p>A shot metered at 1/125s (0.008s) unfiltered, with an ND1000 filter (10 stops), becomes 0.008 × 2¹⁰ = about 8.2 seconds — enough to smooth running water or clouds.</p><h2>Limitations</h2><p>The calculation assumes the filter blocks exactly its nominal manufacturer-stated stops — in practice, cheaper ND filters can vary slightly from the nominal value, and may also introduce a slight color cast that needs correcting in post-processing.</p>',
