            'solvedApertureLabel' => 'New equivalent aperture',
            'solvedShutterLabel' => 'New equivalent shutter speed',
            'evShiftLabel' => 'Stop (EV) shift of the changed field',
        ],
        'article' => '<h2>What it\'s for</h2><p>The "exposure triangle" (ISO, aperture and shutter speed) lets you reach the same final brightness in different ways — trading one setting for another that exactly offsets the light difference. This calculator takes the original exposure, the field you\'re about to change, and the field that should compensate for that change, then calculates the new value needed to keep an equivalent exposure.</p><h2>How the calculation works</h2><p>Each setting is converted into EV stops relative to its original value: <code>ISO_stops = log₂(new_ISO / original_ISO)</code>; <code>aperture_stops = 2 · log₂(new_aperture / original_aperture)</code> (the factor of 2 exists because aperture changes by √2 per stop, not by 2×, since the aperture area — which determines light — is proportional to the square of the f-number); <code>shutter_stops = log₂(new_speed / original_speed)</code>. After finding how many stops the changed field gained or lost, we apply the inverse shift to the chosen field, keeping total exposure constant.</p><h2>Worked example</h2><p>If you had ISO 100, f/8, 1/100s, and open up to f/4 (opening from f/8 to f/4 gains 4 stops of light), the equivalent shutter speed needs to compensate by losing 4 stops — going from 1/100s to about 1/1600s.</p><h2>Why this is useful</h2><p>It lets you trade depth of field for shutter speed (or vice versa) without recalculating by hand, or simulate how an ISO change would affect a scene\'s light requirements — essential when adapting a reference photo\'s settings to different conditions.</p><h2>Limitations</h2><p>The calculation assumes a perfect reciprocity relationship between the three parameters, valid for the vast majority of situations — extremely long exposures (minutes) can suffer a small reciprocity deviation on some sensors, not captured here.</p>',
