        'article' => '<h2>What it\'s for</h2><p>A timelapse compresses a long event (a sunset, something being built, cloud movement) into a short video by taking a photo at a fixed interval. This calculator solves the two most common scenarios: finding the interval and number of shots needed for a video of a target duration, or finding how much event time a given shot interval will cover.</p><h2>Formulas used</h2><p>From video duration: the total shots needed is <code>total_shots = video_duration_in_seconds × fps</code>, and the interval between each shot is <code>interval = event_duration_in_seconds / total_shots</code>. In reverse, from a fixed interval: <code>total_shots = event_duration_in_seconds / interval</code>, and the resulting video duration is <code>video_duration = total_shots / fps</code>.</p><h2>Worked example</h2><p>A 2-hour sunset (7,200 seconds), compressed into a 10-second video at 24fps, needs 240 shots (10×24), taken every 30 seconds (7,200÷240).</p><h2>Practical tips</h2><p>Fast-moving events (clouds, traffic) typically use short intervals (1-5 seconds); slow events (construction, plant growth) use intervals of minutes to hours. Also check that your camera\'s battery and memory card can handle the total number of shots calculated before starting the capture.</p><h2>Limitations</h2><p>The calculation doesn\'t account for camera settling time between shots (relevant at very short intervals with slower cameras) nor storage space needed, since that varies a lot by camera and file format.</p>',
