            'flightDurationLabel' => 'Flight duration',
        ],
        'article' => '<h2>What it is for</h2><p>Tickets show departure in the origin city\'s time and arrival in the destination city\'s time, and doing that math in your head with time zones and daylight saving is easy to get wrong. Enter the departure (local date and time) and the flight duration and this tool returns the local arrival date and time, plus the same moments in UTC.</p><h2>How the calculation works</h2><p>The local departure time is converted to UTC using the official IANA time zone of the origin city on that date, with one extra verification pass to settle the days when daylight saving starts or ends. We then add the flight duration, which is the same in every time zone, and convert the resulting instant to the destination\'s zone. Formula: <code>arrival UTC = departure UTC + duration</code>; <code>local arrival = arrival UTC + destination offset</code>.</p><h2>Worked example</h2><p>Departing São Paulo (UTC−3) on 15 July at 22:00 on an 11 h 30 min flight to Lisbon: departure is 01:00 UTC on the 16th, arrival is 12:30 UTC and, since Lisbon is on UTC+1 in the European summer, you land at <strong>13:30 on 16 July</strong>. The same flight in December lands at 12:30, because Lisbon is on UTC+0. New York to London crosses the date line of the clock: leaving at 19:00 in July on a 7 h flight arrives at 07:00 the next day.</p><h2>The International Date Line</h2><p>Flights across the Pacific can arrive &quot;before&quot; they left or on an unexpected calendar day. A Sydney to Los Angeles flight leaving at 10:00 and lasting 13 hours lands at 06:00 on the same calendar day, for example. The &quot;day relative to departure&quot; line shows +1, 0 or −1 to make this clear.</p><h2>Limits</h2><p>The flight duration is something you enter, so the result is only as good as the airline\'s estimate, and delays are not modeled. If the local departure time falls in the gap that does not exist when clocks spring forward, it is resolved to an adjacent valid instant (one hour off). If a country changes its time zone rules after your browser\'s tables were published, small differences are possible until the system updates.</p>',
        'faq' => [
