    'en' => [
        'title' => 'Sleep Cycle Calculator: Bedtime and Wake-Up Time | uToolsGo',
        'meta' => 'Find when to go to bed or wake up based on 90-minute sleep cycles, adding the minutes it takes you to fall asleep. Free, runs in your browser.',
        'h1' => 'Sleep Cycle Calculator',
        'subtitle' => 'Enter when you need to wake up (or when you are going to bed) to see suggested times that complete full 90-minute sleep cycles.',
        'fieldLabels' => [
            'scMode' => 'What do you want to calculate?',
            'scTime' => 'Time',
            'scFallAsleep' => 'Minutes to fall asleep',
        ],
        'optionLabels' => [
            'wakeAt' => 'I need to wake up at… (suggest bedtimes)',
            'bedAt' => 'I\'m going to bed at… (suggest wake-up times)',
        ],
        'strings' => [
            'error' => 'Enter a valid time and a time to fall asleep between 0 and 180 minutes.',
            'bedtimesLabel' => 'Suggested bedtimes',
            'wakeTimesLabel' => 'Suggested wake-up times',
            'colBedtime' => 'Go to bed',
            'colWake' => 'Wake up',
            'colCycles' => 'Cycles',
            'colSleep' => 'Total sleep',
            'tagRecommended' => 'usual target',
            'tagShort' => 'short',
            'cycleNote' => 'Calculated with an average 90-minute cycle. Real cycle length varies between people and through the night (roughly 70 to 110 min), so use this as a starting point and adjust to how you feel.',
        ],
        'article' => '<h2>What it is for</h2><p>Sleep is not a flat line: it alternates lighter and deeper stages, and REM sleep, in cycles. Many people feel better waking at the end of a cycle than in the middle of one. This calculator suggests bedtimes (if you know your wake-up time) or wake-up times (if you know when you will lie down) so that your sleep adds up to a whole number of 90-minute cycles.</p><h2>Formulas</h2><p>To wake at a given time: <code>bedtime = wake time − cycles × 90 min − minutes to fall asleep</code>. To go to bed at a given time: <code>wake time = bedtime + minutes to fall asleep + cycles × 90 min</code>. We show 6, 5, 4 and 3 cycles (9 h, 7 h 30 min, 6 h and 4 h 30 min of sleep). The math crosses midnight normally and times appear in 24-hour format.</p><h2>Where 90 comes from</h2><p>In young adults, the sleep literature describes cycles of roughly 90 minutes, and that is the average used in estimates like this one. In practice cycle length varies, roughly from 70 to 110 minutes, between people and across the night (early cycles tend to hold more deep sleep and later ones more REM). That is why the result is an estimate, not a measurement of your own sleep.</p><h2>How many cycles?</h2><p>Five to six cycles (7 h 30 min to 9 h) match the 7 to 9 hour range that organizations such as the National Sleep Foundation recommend for most adults. Three cycles (4 h 30 min) appear as a minimum option, marked short: it suits an exceptional night, not a habit.</p><h2>Worked example</h2><p>To wake at 07:00 with 15 minutes to fall asleep: 6 cycles call for lights-out at 21:45; 5 cycles at 23:15; 4 cycles at 00:45; 3 cycles at 02:15. Going to bed at 23:00 with the same 15 minutes: 5 cycles lead to 06:45 and 6 cycles to 08:15.</p><h2>Limits</h2><p>This is not a diagnosis and does not replace medical advice. If you sleep enough hours but often wake up tired, snore heavily or feel excessively sleepy during the day, it is worth talking to a health professional.</p>',
        'faq' => [
            [
                'q' => 'Why 90 minutes?',
                'a' => 'It is the average length of a sleep cycle in adults, used as a reference. The real value varies from person to person and from night to night.',
