        'article' => '<h2>What it is for</h2><p>The amount of sleep we need changes across the lifespan. This calculator shows the recommended range of sleep hours per day for your age, from newborn to older adult, using the table published by the National Sleep Foundation (NSF) in 2015.</p><h2>The table used</h2><table><thead><tr><th>Age group</th><th>Hours per day</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Newborn (0 to 3 months)</td><td>14 to 17</td></tr><tr><td>Infant (4 to 11 months)</td><td>12 to 15</td></tr><tr><td>Toddler (1 to 2 years)</td><td>11 to 14</td></tr><tr><td>Preschooler (3 to 5 years)</td><td>10 to 13</td></tr><tr><td>School-age child (6 to 13 years)</td><td>9 to 11</td></tr><tr><td>Teenager (14 to 17 years)</td><td>8 to 10</td></tr><tr><td>Young adult (18 to 25 years)</td><td>7 to 9</td></tr><tr><td>Adult (26 to 64 years)</td><td>7 to 9</td></tr><tr><td>Older adult (65 years or more)</td><td>7 to 8</td></tr></tbody></table><h2>Source and cross-check</h2><p>The numbers come from the NSF report published in the journal Sleep Health in 2015 (Hirshkowitz et al.), produced by a multidisciplinary expert panel, and match the table on the NSF website. Other bodies, such as the US CDC and the American Academy of Sleep Medicine, use slightly different age bands and values (for example 12 to 16 hours for infants aged 4 to 12 months and 9 to 12 hours for children aged 6 to 12). Treat the values as a reference, not an exact rule.</p><h2>Worked example</h2><p>A 30-year-old falls in the adult band (26 to 64): 7 to 9 hours. A 15-year-old teenager: 8 to 10 hours. A 6-month-old infant: 12 to 15 hours, including naps.</p><h2>Limits</h2><p>The NSF notes that an hour or two more or less may be appropriate for some people. This is general guidance, not medical advice: genetics, health, routine and lifestyle all affect individual need. If you have concerns about your own sleep or a child\'s, talk to a health professional.</p>',
